# highlight — the role projection: the ONE role taxonomy, the cards that refine
# it, and the scanner that projects a surface into both.
#
# CLAUDE.md §0d and §0g are the law this file implements. The claim, and the
# one thing worth reading it to check:
#
#     H-1 COLOUR FOLLOWS THE EDGE, NOT THE GLYPH.
#
# A highlighter that is a lexer cannot make that claim, because a lexer sees
# glyphs. The two places where the difference is visible from outside are the
# two glyphs idol overloads, and both are in the corpus:
#
#   `:`  is TWO roles.  `x: f64` is the COPULA — the ascription edge, law/red.
#                       `lx:read` is INVOKE — the call edge, callable/purple.
#   `|`  is TWO roles.  `t | error` is UNION — a descriptor edge, orange.
#                       `sh "x" | grep("y")` is PIPE — data flow, green.
#
# HOW EACH IS DECIDED, stated so the shortcut is auditable:
#
#   `:`  by grammar.md R1, WHICH IS NORMATIVE: INVOKE always takes
#        an argument group, IS never does. This file used to decide it by
#        LAYOUT — §0d's "copula SPACED, invoke TIGHT" — and gaps/GAP-078.md
#        ruled that reading a FINDING and deleted it. The argument that lost is
#        kept here because it was serious: the renderer is normative, so
#        spacing is a fact about the graph that survives into the byte stream.
#        What breaks the tie is TOTALITY. Spacing survives only for input the
#        formatter has already touched, and H-6 makes the role function total —
#        it must answer for hand-typed, mid-edit, pasted and foreign input too.
#        R1 answers there; spacing does not. So `count:u32` is a COPULA: it is
#        tight, and it carries no call group. §0d's spacing sentence is a
#        RENDERING consequence on canonical input, never the decider.
#
#   `|`  by EDGE STATE, and it cannot be done any other way. The scan carries
#        one bit — am I on a descriptor edge? — and that bit is set BY THE
#        COPULA. So the second proof is downstream of the first: `|` in
#        `(lx, b): u64 | error` is a union because the `:` a few bytes earlier
#        was a copula, and `|` in `sh "x" | grep("y")` is a pipe because
#        nothing on that line opened a descriptor edge. No glyph window of any
#        width answers this. The roles CHAIN, which is the whole thesis.
#
# ── G-TOTAL (§0g) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Three obligations, and the third is the sharp one:
#
#   COVERAGE      every non-whitespace byte carries a role. Checked by length:
#                 a golden role line must be exactly as long as its source
#                 line, so an unassigned byte cannot be spelled.
#
#   NO GUESSES    unresolved-ambiguity spans = 0. Two candidates is a
#                 MIXED-SPACE DIAGNOSTIC, never a default. This file emits the
#                 role `!` for such a span — not a fallback colour, an absence
#                 of decision that the gate counts and the corpus forbids. A
#                 fallback role papering over an undecidable span is exactly
#                 what G-TOTAL exists to catch, so the two places this scanner
#                 genuinely cannot decide (`caseat`, and `;`) DIAGNOSE. The
#                 negative-control fixture proves the machinery fires; a
#                 "0 unresolved" report over a corpus that cannot produce one
#                 is the kind of zero §3 says is usually a broken gate.
#
#   WHY-CHAIN     every role carries its rule citation and dnir edge. `why()`
#                 answers from the same rows the projection uses.
#
# SPECIFICITY is measured SEPARATELY and is the bar coverage is not.
# A taxonomy that assigns every byte a role but tells you nothing a lexer could
# not have told you scores 100% coverage and FAILS. The measure: a lexeme whose
# (role, card) is the SAME everywhere it appears was decided by its spelling —
# the lexer could have done it. A lexeme that takes two or more (role, card)
# pairs across the corpus was decided by its EDGE. The gate reports the split.
#
# ── CLOSURE IS THE LAW; THE COUNT NEVER WAS (gaps/GAP-078.md, ruled) ────────
#
# This file used to assert "H-2 closes the taxonomy at 18" and check it. It no
# longer asserts any number, and neither does `docs/spec/roles.md`, because the
# rendered spec states H-2 as *"the role set is closed"* — with NO number. "18"
# was a summary artifact. The RULING is CLOSURE: finite, enumerated, TOTAL. The
# count is a BINDING, and a binding is checked against what the renderer needs
# rather than defended. `closed()` below now checks closure — well-formedness,
# uniqueness, totality of the hue/rule/capture/card columns, and the presence of
# the diagnostic member — and reports the count without ruling on it.
#
# That dissolves the 17-vs-18 disagreement rather than resolving it: two correct
# enumerations of different working sets were never a conflict.
#
# ── ROLES vs CARDS: THE DISCRIMINATOR IS HUE, EXCEPT WHERE THE LAW NAMES ────
#
# §0g makes fine splits LAW: `=` binds vs `+=` updates vs `==` relates; level
# parens vs parameter parens vs grouping; descriptor braces; walk-writes; comma
# stratifies; unit `*`; dnir `=`/`->`; per-language leftovers. Those are
# REFINEMENTS WITHIN roles, carried here as a second column: a CARD. `bind`,
# `update` and `relation` are three cards of the ONE role `operator`; `strata`,
# `param` and `group` are three cards of `layout`. THE DISCRIMINATOR IS HUE:
# H-1's own splits change the colour — copula red, invoke purple; union orange,
# pipe green — and H-4 defines a role's hue as its semantic space. `=`, `+=`
# and `==` never change colour, so they refine rather than fork.
#
# WHERE THE LAW NAMES A ROLE, THE LAW WINS OVER THAT DISCRIMINATOR. §4
# names SPACE WALK (`to[str]`) its own ROLE, descriptor-tinted — trie navigation
# into descriptor space, not a bracket. The hue discriminator would have made it
# a card inside `descriptor`; it is a role because the law says so, and it is
# spelled `retrieve` (§4's own word for `[ ]`). Braces (a matching
# stack), comma (stratify), unit `*` and dnir `=`/`->` stay CARDS, which is what
# the same section says about them.
#
# ── THE OTHER TAXONOMY IS GONE, NOT PARKED BESIDE THIS ONE ──────────────────
#
# `docs/spec/roles.md` + `scripts/role_scan.id` landed the same day over this
# same corpus directory with a second, disagreeing taxonomy. GAP-078 ruled and
# `scripts/role_scan.id` is DELETED; `docs/spec/roles.md` is now the prose face
# of the table below rather than a rival enumeration. What that document won is
# carried here and is not a compromise:
#
#   `:`         decided by grammar.md R1, above. Its §5.1 was right.
#   `literal`   the string role's spelling. No argument ran either way, so the
#               tracked spec's word is taken; this file used to say `text`.
#   ambiguous   a taxonomy with no way to SAY "two candidates" cannot report
#               the number G-TOTAL gates. The member is `!`, it is NOT a role,
#               it is never a colour, and `closed()` requires it to exist.
#
# What this file won, and keeps: `place` — the ordinary binding, blue, heavy at
# definition. roles.md called it `name` and left it UNHUED, calling it "the
# largest hole"; an unhued role cannot satisfy H-6's total function, which is
# exactly why one implementation measured 20% coverage and this one 100%.
#
# Four of roles.md's seventeen are merged here rather than carried: `opcode` and
# `mnemonic` fold into `callable` (an opcode and a method call are the same
# edge, which is the ONE-COLOUR-LANGUAGE claim), `register` folds into `place`
# (§0b: "registers are places"), and `witness` folds into `walk` (a witness
# reference is a walk into provenance). `foreign` and `comment` are added, the
# first because H-5 makes the trust boundary typographic and a span with no
# graph behind it cannot borrow a role from one, the second because H-6 forces
# it — comment bytes are non-whitespace. Every one of those moves is a
# judgement, and each is recoverable: they are ROWS in the table below, and the
# corpus re-measures the moment a row changes.
#
# ── WHAT THIS IS NOT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Not the graph service. §0f makes the graph service the single authority and
# every front-end a projection of it; this file reads the SURFACE and
# reconstructs the edges canonical layout preserves. Where reconstruction is
# impossible the answer is a diagnostic, never a guess. When the graph service
# can answer `roles(file)` directly this becomes its client, and the corpus in
# `fixtures/highlight/` is what proves the swap changed no answer.
#
# H-8 says plain-string output is a finding. The reports here are plain text
# today; recorded as owed, not claimed.
#
# Modes, by env; the path comes from argv:
#   hlpath          the file to project (argv also works when compiled)
#   hlmode=roles    default — one role line per source line
#   hlmode=cards    the same lines, as card codes
#   hlmode=why      + hlrole — H-7: rule, dnir edge and cards behind a role
#   hlmode=legend   the LSP semantic-token legend, generated
#   hlmode=scm      ext/tree-sitter-duo/queries/highlights.scm, generated
#   hlmode=table    the taxonomy, rendered
#
# Exit 0 answered · 2 bad arguments · 3 the taxonomy failed its own closure
# check. A projector with a broken taxonomy must not print a plausible answer.

# ── THE TAXONOMY (H-2: CLOSED — finite, enumerated, total) ───────────────────
#
# ONE table. Each row is a record:
#
#   name  code  hue  weight  italic  luminance  rule  dnir  capture
#
# Fields are whitespace separated and the row ORDER is the legend order — the
# LSP legend indexes into it, so inserting a row renumbers the protocol. That
# is deliberate: the legend is GENERATED, never hand-maintained, so a renumber
# costs nothing, and a hand edit is an H-1 drift finding.
#
# THE FIVE HUES ARE SEMANTIC SPACES (H-4), not decoration:
#   red     law          the rules of the language itself
#   orange  descriptor   what names a shape
#   purple  callable     what can be applied
#   blue    value        what is at rest
#   green   flow         what moves
#
# weight: `heavy` means the role BOLDS AT ITS DEFINITION SITE (H-3, H-5 —
# definition and use share a hue, definition adds weight). `plain` means the
# role has no definition form at all, which is true of every punctuation role.
#
# italic: H-5, AMBIENT and FOREIGN. `world` is italic because an ambient
# capability reach is a fact the reader must see without looking it up, and
# `foreign` is italic because THE TRUST BOUNDARY IS TYPOGRAPHIC — you can see
# where idol stops without reading a word of the span.
#
# luminance: H-6, luminance is SURPRISAL. `faint` is not "less important", it
# is "fully inferable from its surroundings" — layout and comments are the two
# things a reader reconstructs for free. Nothing is ever ABSENT.
#
# dnir: the correspondence H-7 requires, in the vocabulary `src/dnir_lower.zig`
# actually uses. THREE ROLES ANSWER `dnir.none`, and they are exactly `layout`,
# `comment` and `foreign` — the three spans carrying no graph edge. That is not
# a hole in the table, it is the table REPORTING WHERE THE GRAPH ENDS, and it
# lines up with the typography: the two faint roles and the italic one.
#
# capture: the tree-sitter name, and the column where the projection LOSES.
# Several roles land on one capture because tree-sitter has no name for the
# distinction. The loss is counted and printed by the gate, never hidden.
roles = {
  "keyword    k red    plain upright plain h4   dnir.branch @keyword",
  "copula     c red    plain upright plain h1   dnir.ty     @keyword.operator",
  "operator   o red    plain upright plain g17  dnir.binop  @operator",
  "layout     l red    plain upright faint law7 dnir.none   @punctuation.bracket",
  "descriptor d orange heavy upright plain h4   dnir.ty     @type",
  "union      u orange plain upright plain h1   dnir.ty     @punctuation.special",
  "anchor     a orange heavy upright loud  law2 dnir.obj    @attribute",
  "retrieve   r orange plain upright plain p118 dnir.index  @punctuation.bracket",
  "invoke     i purple plain upright plain h1   dnir.method @function.method",
  "callable   f purple heavy upright plain law5 dnir.call   @function",
  "world      w purple plain italic  plain h5   dnir.call   @function.builtin",
  "foreign    g purple plain italic  plain h5   dnir.none   @string.special",
  "place      p blue   heavy upright plain law1 dnir.local  @variable",
  "number     n blue   plain upright plain h4   dnir.const  @number",
  "literal    t blue   plain upright plain law8 dnir.const  @string",
  "case       y blue   plain upright plain law3 dnir.const  @constant.builtin",
  "comment    m blue   plain upright faint h6   dnir.none   @comment",
  "walk       v green  plain upright plain h4   dnir.field  @property",
  "pipe       b green  plain upright plain h1   dnir.call   @operator"
}

# ── THE CARDS (§0g fine splits, REFINEMENTS WITHIN the 18) ───────────────────
#
#   name  role  code  note
#
# A card never carries hue — hue is the role's. A card carries PRECISION: it is
# what the why-chain says beyond the colour, and it is what specificity is
# measured over. `ambiguous` is the one card with no role: it is the mixed-space
# diagnostic G-TOTAL requires in place of a guess, and `closed()` requires it to
# be present, because a taxonomy that cannot SAY "two candidates" cannot report
# the ambiguity number the gate is built to read.
cards = {
  "space    layout     s whitespace and indentation",
  "strata   layout     t level parens, a strata boundary",
  "param    layout     p parameter parens, the argument list",
  "group    layout     g grouping parens",
  "quote    layout     q a literal delimiter",
  "brace    layout     d descriptor braces",
  "stratify layout     x comma, which stratifies a group",
  "walk     retrieve   w a space walk, trie navigation into descriptor space",
  "index    retrieve   i retrieval from a table by a value key",
  "bind     operator   b binds, asserting a fact",
  "update   operator   u an update edge",
  "relation operator   r a relation query",
  "arith    operator   a arithmetic",
  "unit     operator   n a unit product",
  "flow     operator   f a dnir flow edge",
  "read     walk       r a field read",
  "write    walk       w a walk-write, underlined",
  "reach    walk       s a walk through a world",
  "ascribe  copula     a the ascription edge",
  "apply    invoke     y the application edge",
  "alt      union      l an alternative within a descriptor",
  "stream   pipe       m a demand stream",
  "shape    descriptor h a shape",
  "nonterm  descriptor n a nonterminal reference",
  "slot     descriptor s a declared slot at a descriptor",
  "control  keyword    k control law",
  "bare     anchor     d the current world",
  "rel      anchor     n a postfix anchor",
  "call     callable   c an application",
  "opcode   callable   o a machine or dnir operation",
  "ambient  world      w an ambient capability reach",
  "opaque   foreign    g foreign source, uncoloured within",
  "value    place      v a named value at rest",
  "reg      place      r a machine register",
  "fact     place      f a dnir fact name",
  "lit      number     n a numeric literal",
  "imm      number     i a machine immediate",
  "quoted   literal    q a string literal",
  "term     literal    t an ebnf terminal",
  "inferred case       y an inferred case",
  "prose    comment    m prose",
  "meta     comment    d dnir metadata notation",
  "ambiguous none      ! two candidates, so a mixed-space diagnostic"
}

# github dark (Primer), mapped over HUE, not over role. §0d fixes the theme;
# the point of the taxonomy is that a theme is a lookup table with five entries
# and one dim, not eighteen hand-picked colours. Swapping themes is swapping
# this table and nothing else.
hues = {
  "red    ff7b72:len()",
  "orange ffa657:len()",
  "purple d2a8ff:len()",
  "blue   #79c0ff",
  "green  #7ee787",
  "faint  #8b949e"
}

# The rule citations, expanded. H-7 wants the CITATION, not an identifier that
# means nothing outside this file.
rules = {
  "h1   §0d H-1 colour follows the edge, not the glyph",
  "h4   §0d H-4 hue is the semantic space",
  "h5   §0d H-5 weight is definition; italic is ambient or foreign",
  "h6   §0d H-6 the role function is total; luminance is surprisal",
  "g17  §0g G-TOTAL fine splits are law; a card refines, never forks, a role",
  "p118 §0d §4 the space walk is trie navigation, its own role",
  "law1 §0.1 LAW-ONE identifiers are one lowercase word",
  "law2 §0.2 the @ dyad: bare descriptor and postfix anchor",
  "law3 §0.3 inferred cases, read from descriptor-expected position",
  "law5 §0.5 FACE-CALL declare at the trie, call at the value",
  "law7 §0.7 layout is offside, canonical, verified rendering",
  "law8 §0.8 strings interpolate; a byte is a byte"
}

# ── THE TREE-SITTER BINDINGS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# node pattern -> ROLE NAME. The capture is looked up in the taxonomy, so the
# query file is generated from (taxonomy x bindings) and a role's capture can
# be changed in exactly one place. ORDER IS SIGNIFICANT and is preserved:
# editors resolve overlapping captures by the LAST match, so general patterns
# come first and specific ones after. That ordering is the one thing here that
# is not derivable, so it is written down.
#
# These patterns are AUTHORED against the grammar's node names, and that is the
# seam: the grammar is not derived from the taxonomy and cannot be until it has
# an offside scanner (see scripts/treesitter_emit.id). Named, not hidden.
bindings = {
  "(identifier) place",
  "(named_type) descriptor",
  "(generic_type) descriptor",
  "(array_type) descriptor",
  "(pointer_type) descriptor",
  "(optional_type) descriptor",
  "(function_type) descriptor",
  "(record_type) descriptor",
  "(binding_name type: (type) CAP) descriptor",
  "(parameter type: (type) CAP) descriptor",
  "(type_parameter (identifier) CAP) descriptor",
  "(enum_definition (identifier) CAP) descriptor",
  "(concept_definition (identifier) CAP) descriptor",
  "(alias_definition (identifier) CAP) descriptor",
  "(integer) number",
  "(float) number",
  "(nil) case",
  "(boolean) case",
  "(enum_variant (identifier) CAP) case",
  "(double_quoted_string) literal",
  "(single_quoted_string) literal",
  "(long_string) literal",
  "(function_declaration name: (function_name) CAP) callable",
  "(bare_function_declaration name: (function_name) CAP) callable",
  "(foreign_declaration name: (function_name) CAP) callable",
  "(function_expression) callable",
  "(function_value_expression) callable",
  "(parameter (identifier) CAP) place",
  "(binding_pattern (identifier) CAP) place",
  "(rest_pattern (identifier) CAP) place",
  "(label_statement (identifier) CAP) place",
  "(goto_statement (identifier) CAP) place",
  "(field_expression (identifier) CAP) walk",
  "(field_projection (identifier) CAP) walk",
  "(table_entry . (identifier) CAP) walk",
  "(table_pattern_entry (identifier) CAP) walk",
  "(descriptor_constructor) anchor",
  "(attribute) anchor",
  "(vararg) layout",
  "(empty_statement) layout",
  "(shebang) comment",
  "(break_statement) keyword",
  "(compound_assignment) operator",
  "(unary_expression) operator",
  "(binary_expression) operator"
}

# The keyword sets. `legacy` is the pre-Pass-100 surface the tracked corpus is
# still mostly written in: retiring a spelling from the LANGUAGE does not stop
# an editor needing to colour the 700-odd files that still carry it, and a
# query file that drops them makes the corpus look broken in every editor
# before it is repaired in any of them.
legacy = "local global const function fun goto match case catch try defer await enum concept alias extends private comptime"

# ── field access over the record rows ────────────────────────────────────────

# Field `n` of a whitespace-separated record. Answers "" past the end, which
# every caller treats as a failure rather than as an empty field.
field: str = (line: str, n: i64)
  out: str = ""
  seen: i64 = 0
  i: i64 = 1
  inword: bool = false
  start: i64 = 0
  while i <= line:len()
    b: i64 = line:byte(i)
    if b == 32 or b == 9
      if inword
        seen = seen + 1
        if seen == n
          out = line:sub(start, i - 1)
        inword = false
    else
      if not inword
        inword = true
        start = i
    i = i + 1
  if inword
    seen = seen + 1
    if seen == n
      out = line:sub(start, line:len())
  out

# The role row whose name or code is `key`; "" when absent.
rowof: str = (key: str)
  out: str = ""
  i: i64 = 1
  while i <= roles:len()
    r: str = roles(i)
    if field(r, 1) == key or field(r, 2) == key
      out = r
    i = i + 1
  out

# code to name and name to code, over the SAME rows. H-7 asks for provenance
# maps that are bidirectional DATA; two directions over one table is what that
# means in practice, because there is no second table to drift.
naming: str = (code: str)
  field(rowof(code), 1)

coding: str = (name: str)
  field(rowof(name), 2)

# The card name for a (role code, card code) pair.
carding: str = (role: str, card: str)
  out: str = ""
  nm: str = naming(role)
  i: i64 = 1
  while i <= cards:len()
    c: str = cards(i)
    if field(c, 3) == card and (field(c, 2) == nm or field(c, 2) == "none")
      out = field(c, 1)
    i = i + 1
  out

hexof: str = (hue: str)
  out: str = ""
  i: i64 = 1
  while i <= hues:len()
    if field(hues(i), 1) == hue
      out = field(hues(i), 2)
    i = i + 1
  out

citing: str = (id: str)
  out: str = ""
  i: i64 = 1
  while i <= rules:len()
    r: str = rules(i)
    if field(r, 1) == id
      out = r:sub(id:len() + 1, r:len())
      while out:len() > 0 and out:byte(1) == 32
        out = out:sub(2, out:len())
    i = i + 1
  script.chomp(out)

# CLOSURE, checked before anything is printed. H-2 rules that the role set is
# CLOSED — finite, enumerated, total — and rules NO NUMBER, so no number is
# asserted here. What is checked is closure itself: the set is non-empty, every
# row is well formed, no name and no code is claimed twice, every role carries a
# hue a hue row defines, a rule a rule row expands, a tree-sitter capture and at
# least one card, every card refines a role that exists, and the DIAGNOSTIC
# member is present. A projector whose taxonomy has drifted must not answer at
# all, because its answers would look exactly like correct ones. Positive-
# controlled by the gate, which corrupts a copy of these tables and requires
# this to fire.
closed: str = ()
  bad: str = ""
  if roles:len() == 0
    bad = "the taxonomy is empty; a closed set is enumerated, not absent"
  i: i64 = 1
  while i <= roles:len()
    r: str = roles(i)
    nm: str = field(r, 1)
    cd: str = field(r, 2)
    if cd:len() != 1
      bad = "role {nm} has code [{cd}], which is not one byte"
    if hexof(field(r, 3)) == ""
      bad = "role {nm} claims a hue no hue row defines"
    if citing(field(r, 7)) == ""
      bad = "role {nm} cites a rule no rule row expands"
    if field(r, 9) == ""
      bad = "role {nm} has no tree-sitter capture"
    j: i64 = 1
    while j <= roles:len()
      if j != i and field(roles(j), 2) == cd
        bad = "role code {cd} is claimed twice"
      if j != i and field(roles(j), 1) == nm
        bad = "role name {nm} appears twice"
      j = j + 1
    seen: i64 = 0
    j = 1
    while j <= cards:len()
      if field(cards(j), 2) == nm
        seen = seen + 1
      j = j + 1
    if seen == 0
      bad = "role {nm} carries no card; §0g wants every role refined"
    i = i + 1
  i = 1
  diag: i64 = 0
  while i <= cards:len()
    c: str = cards(i)
    cn: str = field(c, 1)
    cr: str = field(c, 2)
    if cr != "none" and rowof(cr) == ""
      bad = "card {cn} refines {cr}, which is not a role"
    if cr == "none" and field(c, 3) == kbad
      diag = diag + 1
    i = i + 1
  # gap[078]: the taxonomy must be able to SAY "two candidates". G-TOTAL gates
  # ambiguity at 0, and a set with no diagnostic member reports that 0 by
  # being unable to count rather than by deciding — the confident zero §3
  # names. Losing this member is losing the number.
  if diag != 1
    n: str = to(str)(diag)
    bad = "the taxonomy carries {n} diagnostic members; G-TOTAL needs exactly one"
  bad

# ── byte predicates ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

blank: bool = (b: i64)
  b == 32 or b == 9

digit: bool = (b: i64)
  b >= 48 and b <= 57

heady: bool = (b: i64)
  b >= 97 and b <= 122 or b >= 65 and b <= 90 or b == 95

wordy: bool = (b: i64)
  heady(b) or digit(b)

# The next byte at or after `i` that is not a space; 0 at end of line.
ahead: i64 = (line: str, i: i64)
  j: i64 = i
  out: i64 = 0
  while j <= line:len() and out == 0
    b: i64 = line:byte(j)
    if not blank(b)
      out = b
    j = j + 1
  out

# The INDEX of the next byte at or after `i` that is not a space; 0 past the end.
nextat: i64 = (line: str, i: i64)
  j: i64 = i
  out: i64 = 0
  while j <= line:len() and out == 0
    if not blank(line:byte(j))
      out = j
    j = j + 1
  out

# The byte at `i`, or 0 off the end.
byteat: i64 = (line: str, i: i64)
  out: i64 = 0
  if i >= 1 and i <= line:len()
    out = line:byte(i)
  out

# The previous byte at or before `i` that is not a space; 0 at start of line.
behind: i64 = (line: str, i: i64)
  j: i64 = i
  out: i64 = 0
  while j >= 1 and out == 0
    b: i64 = line:byte(j)
    if not blank(b)
      out = b
    j = j - 1
  out

# Membership in a space-separated set. One string rather than a table so the
# sets stay checkable by eye against the law they come from.
holds: bool = (set: str, word: str)
  found: bool = false
  for i, w in set:split(" ")
    if w == word
      found = true
  found

# Does `s` contain a literal dot? `find` is pattern-matching — `s:find(".")`
# answers 1 for every non-empty string — so this is a byte scan on purpose.
dotted: bool = (s: str)
  found: bool = false
  i: i64 = 1
  while i <= s:len()
    if s:byte(i) == 46
      found = true
    i = i + 1
  found

keywords = "if then else elseif while for in do end and or not return break repeat until use"
literals = "nil true false"
# The primitives are descriptors WHEREVER they stand, not only after a copula:
# `any(i64, f64)` puts them in argument position and they are still shapes.
shapes = "i64 i32 i16 i8 u64 u32 u16 u8 f64 f32 str bool void any error byte bit"
# §0a RECOGNITION: worlds are the only capability namespaces, so this set is
# the ambient reach — and it is exactly what H-5 renders italic.
worlds = "print sh io os fs net clock rand env std task"

# The codes, bound once. Spelling a role or card code as a bare letter at a
# call site is how a taxonomy and its consumers drift apart — and idol has no
# string literal inside an interpolation hole, so binding them is also the only
# way the scanner can name them where it uses them.
mcode = "m"
vcode = "v"
ycode = "y"
ncode = "n"
pcode = "p"
ocode = "o"
acode = "a"
lcode = "l"
tcode = "t"
rcode = "r"
kprose = "m"
kmeta = "d"
krel = "r"
kcase = "y"
kimm = "i"
kreg = "r"
klit = "n"
kflow = "f"
# NOT a role. The mixed-space diagnostic G-TOTAL requires in place of a guess.
kbad = "!"

# ── the projector ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The scan emits INTERLEAVED pairs: role byte, then card byte, per source byte.
# One pass produces both, so a role and its card can never disagree about what
# was scanned — which is the failure mode a second pass would introduce.

pairof: str = (role: str, card: str, n: i64)
  acc: str = ""
  i: i64 = 0
  while i < n
    acc = "{acc}{role}{card}"
    i = i + 1
  acc

# Take every other byte of an interleaved string: `k` = 1 for roles, 2 for cards.
lane: str = (duet: str, k: i64)
  out: str = ""
  i: i64 = k
  while i <= duet:len()
    c: str = duet:sub(i, i)
    out = out .. c
    i = i + 2
  out

# The identifier starting at `i`.
wordat: str = (line: str, i: i64)
  j: i64 = i
  while j <= line:len() and wordy(line:byte(j))
    j = j + 1
  line:sub(i, j - 1)

# LADDERED: does this line spell a strata LEVEL? §0.5 declares operation-first
# at the trie (`read(number) = (lx, b)…`) and calls subject-first at the value
# (`lx:read(number)(b)`), and BOTH mark the level the same way — by there being
# a SECOND paren group right after the first. A level is the MARKED form, and
# this is the mark. So `f(x)` is arguments and `read(number)(b)` is a level then
# arguments, decided on the surface rather than guessed at.
laddered: bool = (line: str)
  found: bool = false
  i: i64 = 1
  while i < line:len()
    if line:byte(i) == 41
      nx: i64 = ahead(line, i + 1)
      if nx == 40
        found = true
      if nx == 61 and ahead(line, i + 3) == 40
        found = true
    i = i + 1
  found

# The role and card for an identifier, given what surrounds it. This is where a
# lexer and a projection part company: the same bytes take four roles, and the
# order of these arms is the priority the law implies.
wordrole: str = (line: str, i: i64, word: str, descpos: bool, prev: i64, inbrace: bool)
  out: str = "pv"
  nxt: i64 = ahead(line, i + word:len())
  if holds(keywords, word)
    out = "kk"
  else(holds(literals, word))
    out = "yy"
  else(holds(shapes, word))
    out = "dh"
  else(holds(worlds, word))
    out = "ww"
  else(descpos)
    # ON A DESCRIPTOR EDGE, and this arm must outrank the invoke arm below.
    # Both are "the previous byte was a colon"; only the EDGE separates
    # them, and `descpos` is exactly the bit the copula set. Ordered the
    # other way round, `open(path) = (p): file | error` read `file` as a
    # called name because a colon preceded it — a lexer's answer, and the
    # corpus caught it. `x: seq(entry)` names a shape and `seq` is its head.
    out = "dh"
  else(prev == 58)
    # reached through an INVOKE `:`; the receiver is to the left. §0.5.
    out = "fc"
  else(nxt == 123)
    # immediately before `{`: rung (a) of the CONSTRUCTOR LADDER, §0.4 —
    # `desc{…}`. The name heads a descriptor, it is not a value.
    out = "dh"
  else(inbrace and nxt == 61)
    # a name inside a descriptor brace, bound: that is a SLOT declaration,
    # §0.3's case-set inline at the field. It names part of a shape, so it
    # is a descriptor with the slot card, not a place.
    out = "ds"
  else(nxt == 40)
    # immediately before `(`: an application. §0.5 — the trie declares
    # operation-first and the value calls subject-first, and BOTH spellings
    # put the operation immediately before its parenthesis.
    out = "fc"
  out

# A leading `.` is the one place the surface genuinely underdetermines the
# graph, and G-TOTAL forbids guessing there.
#
# §0.3 infers a case FROM DESCRIPTOR-EXPECTED POSITION; §0a says a leading
# `.name` reads the subject's fields. Both are leading dots. What separates
# them is where a descriptor is expected, which is a graph fact, not a layout
# one. What CAN be read off the surface is the operator to the left: after `=`,
# `(`, `,` or `|` a descriptor is expected and the dot opens a CASE; after a
# word or `)` or `]` the dot is a WALK. At the head of a clause it is neither
# decidable nor defaultable, so it DIAGNOSES — §0g, two candidates is a
# mixed-space diagnostic.
caseat: bool = (prev: i64)
  prev == 61 or prev == 40 or prev == 44 or prev == 124

walkat: bool = (prev: i64)
  wordy(prev) or prev == 41 or prev == 93

# Project one line into interleaved role/card bytes. `surface` selects the
# reading; `idol` is the real one and the other four are the surfaces §0d
# requires ONE colour language to cover.
# A foreign fixture is a idol file that CARRIES foreign source; its own header
# and prose are idol and sit OUTSIDE the trust boundary the header declares.
# Only a line that starts with `--` is frame — mid-line `--` (C's `n--`) is
# foreign text and stays foreign.
framing: bool = (line: str)
  i: i64 = 1
  while i <= line:len() and blank(line:byte(i))
    i = i + 1
  i + 1 <= line:len() and line:byte(i) == 45 and line:byte(i + 1) == 45

duet: str = (line: str, surface: str)
  out: str = ""
  if surface == "foreign" and not framing(line)
    # §0d H-5: the trust boundary is typographic. There is no graph behind
    # foreign source — that is what makes it foreign — so it takes ONE role
    # across its whole extent and the italic says where idol stopped.
    # Colouring inside it would be a claim to understand it.
    i: i64 = 1
    while i <= line:len()
      if blank(line:byte(i))
        out = "{out}ls"
      else
        out = "{out}gg"
      i = i + 1
  else
    out = scan(line, surface)
  out

project: str = (line: str, surface: str)
  lane(duet(line, surface), 1)

carded: str = (line: str, surface: str)
  lane(duet(line, surface), 2)

scan: str = (line: str, surface: str)
  out: str = ""
  piece: str = ""
  descpos: bool = false
  head: bool = true
  level: bool = laddered(line)
  depth: i64 = 0
  groups: i64 = 0
  braces: i64 = 0
  opened: str = ""
  holding: str = ""
  streaming: bool = false
  i: i64 = 1
  while i <= line:len()
    b: i64 = line:byte(i)
    nb: i64 = 0
    if i < line:len()
      nb = line:byte(i + 1)
    pb: i64 = behind(line, i - 1)

    if blank(b)
      out = "{out}ls"
      i = i + 1
    else(b == 45 and nb == 45)
      # `--` to end of line. FAINT, never absent (H-6).
      piece = pairof(mcode, kprose, line:len() - i + 1)
      out = out .. piece
      i = line:len() + 1
    else(b == 34 or b == 39)
      # BOTH quotes. `'x'` used to fall through to the arithmetic arm and
      # render as operator/place/operator — a string read as two products
      # around a variable. grammar.md §1 gives `string` both spellings and
      # the flat span corpus is what caught it. gap[078].
      j: i64 = i + 1
      while j <= line:len() and line:byte(j) != b
        j = j + 1
      piece = strand(line, i, j, surface)
      out = out .. piece
      head = false
      i = j + 1
    else(b == 59)
      # THE SEMICOLON DOES NOT EXIST. In idol it names nothing,
      # so it takes no role — it diagnoses. In dnir it is metadata
      # notation, which is a comment, and that is the only reading left.
      if surface == "dnir"
        piece = pairof(mcode, kmeta, line:len() - i + 1)
        out = out .. piece
        i = line:len() + 1
      else
        out = "{out}!!"
        head = false
        i = i + 1
    else(b == 58)
      # THE FIRST PROOF, decided by grammar.md R1 (NORMATIVE):
      # INVOKE always takes an argument group, IS never does. So a `:` is
      # an invoke exactly when a name follows it immediately AND that name
      # is immediately followed by a call group — `(`, `{` or `"`. Every
      # other `:` is the copula, which makes this arm TOTAL: there is no
      # input it declines on, which is precisely why it beats the spacing
      # reading it replaced. `count:u32` is tight, carries no group, and
      # lands here as a COPULA. gap[078].
      if surface == "ebnf"
        out = "{out}ca"
      else
        w: str = wordat(line, i + 1)
        after: i64 = byteat(line, i + 1 + w:len())
        if w:len() > 0 and (after == 40 or after == 123 or after == 34)
          out = "{out}iy"
        else
          out = "{out}ca"
          descpos = true
      head = false
      i = i + 1
    else(b == 124)
      # THE SECOND PROOF, and it reads the bits the earlier arms set.
      # On a descriptor edge it is the UNION. Off the edge §0g gives it
      # two readings and the ruling decides neither: "channels = stream
      # places composed by `|`" is the PIPE, and the retrieval ruling lists `bor`
      # over integer operands. What separates them is operand space, so
      # the only surface fact available is whether a stream place was
      # opened on this line — a world action does that. `sh "ls" | grep(…)`
      # is a pipe because `sh` opened one; `out = xs | ys` has TWO
      # candidates and no rule, so it DIAGNOSES rather than defaulting to
      # green. gap[078]: the ruling did not reach this span.
      if descpos or surface == "ebnf"
        out = "{out}ul"
      else(streaming)
        out = "{out}bm"
      else
        out = "{out}!!"
      head = false
      i = i + 1
    else(b == 61)
      if surface == "ebnf"
        out = "{out}ca"
        i = i + 1
      else(nb == 61)
        # §0g: `==` is a RELATION query, not a binding.
        piece = pairof(ocode, krel, 2)
        out = out .. piece
        i = i + 2
      else(pb == 43 or pb == 45 or pb == 42 or pb == 47)
        out = "{out}ou"
        i = i + 1
      else
        # §0g: `=` BINDS — it asserts a fact — and a binding closes the
        # descriptor edge the copula opened.
        out = "{out}ob"
        descpos = false
        i = i + 1
      head = false
    else(b == 37 and surface == "dnir")
      # BEFORE the arithmetic arm, which also owns byte 37. Tested after
      # it, every dnir place read as a modulo followed by a literal — a
      # plausible-looking answer, which is the kind this corpus exists to
      # catch.
      w: str = wordat(line, i + 1)
      piece = pairof(pcode, kreg, w:len() + 1)
      out = out .. piece
      head = false
      i = i + w:len() + 1
    else(b == 43 or b == 42 or b == 47 or b == 37)
      card: str = "a"
      if nb == 61
        card = "u"
      else(b == 42 and descpos)
        # §0g unit `*`: on a descriptor edge a product is a UNIT, not
        # arithmetic.
        card = "n"
      piece = pairof(ocode, card, 1)
      out = out .. piece
      head = false
      i = i + 1
    else(b == 60 or b == 62)
      out = "{out}or"
      head = false
      i = i + 1
    else(b == 45 and surface == "dnir" and nb == 62)
      piece = pairof(ocode, kflow, 2)
      out = out .. piece
      head = false
      i = i + 2
    else(b == 64)
      # §0.2 the @ DYAD. Postfix on a word is an ANCHOR; otherwise it is
      # the bare innermost descriptor.
      card: str = "d"
      if wordy(pb)
        card = "n"
      piece = pairof(acode, card, 1)
      out = out .. piece
      head = false
      i = i + 1
    else(b == 46)
      w: str = wordat(line, i + 1)
      if walkat(pb)
        # §0g walk-writes: a walk on the left of a binding WRITES.
        # §0g WALK-WRITE. `ahead` answers the BYTE, so testing the
        # byte one past `i + #w + 1` tested the `=` against itself and
        # every write read as a read. `nextat` answers the INDEX, and
        # the byte after it is what separates `=` from `==`.
        card: str = "r"
        k: i64 = nextat(line, i + w:len() + 1)
        if k > 0 and line:byte(k) == 61 and byteat(line, k + 1) != 61
          card = "w"
        if holds(worlds, wordat(line, wordstart(line, i - 1)))
          card = "s"
        piece = pairof(vcode, card, w:len() + 1)
        out = out .. piece
      else(caseat(pb))
        piece = pairof(ycode, kcase, w:len() + 1)
        out = out .. piece
      else
        piece = pairof(kbad, kbad, w:len() + 1)
        out = out .. piece
      head = false
      i = i + w:len() + 1
    else(b == 35 and surface == "asm")
      w: str = wordat(line, i + 1)
      piece = pairof(ncode, kimm, w:len() + 1)
      out = out .. piece
      head = false
      i = i + w:len() + 1
    else(digit(b))
      j: i64 = i
      while j <= line:len() and (digit(line:byte(j)) or line:byte(j) == 46)
        j = j + 1
      piece = pairof(ncode, klit, j - i)
      out = out .. piece
      head = false
      i = j
    else(heady(b))
      w: str = wordat(line, i)
      rc: str = wordrole(line, i, w, descpos, pb, braces > 0)
      if surface == "dnir" and (head or pb == 61)
        # §0d H-4 puts dnir opcodes and asm mnemonics in the CALLABLE
        # space. That is the ONE-COLOUR-LANGUAGE claim doing work: an
        # opcode and a method call are the same edge, so one role — and
        # the card `opcode` is what keeps the surfaces distinguishable.
        rc = "fo"
      if surface == "asm"
        if head
          rc = "fo"
        else(machine(w))
          rc = "pr"
      if surface == "ebnf" and not holds(keywords, w)
        # §0g per-language leftovers: an ebnf leftover is a NONTERMINAL
        # reference, which is a descriptor by another name.
        rc = "dn"
      if surface == "dnir" and rc == "pv"
        # §4, per-language totalization: "every leftover
        # identifier is a NAMED role (`d-i` — a fact name), never
        # generic." A dnir word that reached the generic `value` card
        # got no more than its lexical class, which §0g SPECIFICITY
        # makes a G-TOTAL finding rather than coverage. It is not a
        # value at rest: it NAMES a node in the flattened graph, which
        # is what the `fact` card says and `value` did not.
        rc = "pf"
      if rc == "ww"
        # a world action opens a stream place, which is the one surface
        # fact that separates a PIPE from a `bor` at the `|` arm above.
        streaming = true
      piece = pairof(rc:sub(1, 1), rc:sub(2, 2), w:len())
      out = out .. piece
      head = false
      i = i + w:len()
    else(b == 40)
      depth = depth + 1
      # §0g: level parens vs parameter parens vs grouping. A LEVEL is the
      # marked form and `laddered` is the mark, so on a laddered line the
      # first name-adjacent group is the strata level and the rest are
      # arguments. After `=` a group is the parameter list, which is what
      # `open(path) = (p): …` spells.
      card: str = "g"
      if wordy(pb) or pb == 41
        card = "p"
        if level and groups == 0
          card = "t"
        groups = groups + 1
      else(pb == 61 and level)
        card = "p"
      # the closer must answer what the opener answered, so the card is
      # pushed rather than recomputed; recomputing it read `)` as a level
      # boundary two groups after the level had closed.
      opened = opened .. card
      piece = pairof(lcode, card, 1)
      out = out .. piece
      descpos = false
      head = false
      i = i + 1
    else(b == 41)
      card: str = "g"
      if opened:len() > 0
        card = opened:sub(opened:len(), opened:len())
        opened = opened:sub(1, opened:len() - 1)
      depth = depth - 1
      piece = pairof(lcode, card, 1)
      out = out .. piece
      head = false
      i = i + 1
    else(b == 123 or b == 125)
      if b == 123
        braces = braces + 1
      else
        braces = braces - 1
      out = "{out}ld"
      head = false
      i = i + 1
    else(b == 91)
      # §4: SPACE WALK is its OWN ROLE, descriptor-tinted — trie
      # navigation, not a bracket. `to[str]` walks descriptor space;
      # `xs[0]` retrieves from a table by a value key. What separates them
      # is the CONTENT's space, which is R5's "operands select the
      # operator" read off the surface: a lone shape name is descriptor
      # space, a numeric or quoted key is value space, and a bare name is
      # TWO CANDIDATES — is it a descriptor or a place? — so it diagnoses
      # rather than defaulting. gap[078].
      j: i64 = i + 1
      while j <= line:len() and line:byte(j) != 93
        j = j + 1
      inner: str = line:sub(i + 1, j - 1)
      card: str = kbad
      if holds(shapes, inner)
        card = "w"
      else(inner:len() > 0 and (digit(inner:byte(1)) or inner:byte(1) == 34))
        card = "i"
      role: str = rcode
      if card == kbad
        role = kbad
      holding = holding .. card
      piece = pairof(role, card, 1)
      out = out .. piece
      head = false
      i = i + 1
    else(b == 93)
      # the closer answers what the opener answered, for the reason the
      # paren closer does: recomputing it reads the byte AFTER the group.
      card: str = kbad
      if holding:len() > 0
        card = holding:sub(holding:len(), holding:len())
        holding = holding:sub(1, holding:len() - 1)
      role: str = rcode
      if card == kbad
        role = kbad
      piece = pairof(role, card, 1)
      out = out .. piece
      head = false
      i = i + 1
    else(b == 44)
      # §0g: the comma STRATIFIES a group; it is not decoration.
      out = "{out}lx"
      head = false
      i = i + 1
    else
      out = "{out}oa"
      head = false
      i = i + 1
  out

# Where the word ending at `i` starts.
wordstart: i64 = (line: str, i: i64)
  j: i64 = i
  while j >= 1 and wordy(line:byte(j))
    j = j - 1
  j + 1

# A machine PLACE: §0b, "registers are places". `x0`..`x30`, `w0`.., and the
# named registers all name a place rather than an operation.
machine: bool = (w: str)
  out: bool = false
  if w == "sp" or w == "lr" or w == "pc" or w == "fp"
    out = true
  else(w:len() >= 2 and (w:byte(1) == 120 or w:byte(1) == 119) and digit(w:byte(2)))
    out = true
  out

# A string literal from `i` to its closing quote at `j`. §0.8: interpolation is
# always available, so a hole is NOT string content — it is an expression, and
# the roles reach inside it. Quotes and braces are layout; a hole reads as a
# walk when it carries a dot and as a place otherwise. In ebnf a quoted span is
# a TERMINAL, which is the same role with a different card.
#
# The delimiting quote takes the layout card `quote`, not `group`: it delimits a
# literal and delimits nothing else, and a card that cannot beat "punctuation"
# is the SPECIFICITY finding. The interpolation braces stay `group` —
# they delimit an expression, which is what grouping means.
strand: str = (line: str, i: i64, j: i64, surface: str)
  card: str = "q"
  if surface == "ebnf"
    card = "t"
  out: str = "lq"
  piece: str = ""
  k: i64 = i + 1
  while k <= j - 1
    if line:byte(k) == 123
      e: i64 = k + 1
      while e <= j - 1 and line:byte(e) != 125
        e = e + 1
      inner: str = line:sub(k + 1, e - 1)
      rc: str = "pv"
      if dotted(inner)
        rc = "vr"
      out = "{out}lg"
      piece = pairof(rc:sub(1, 1), rc:sub(2, 2), e - k - 1)
      out = out .. piece
      out = "{out}lg"
      k = e + 1
    else
      piece = pairof(tcode, card, 1)
      out = out .. piece
      k = k + 1
  if j <= line:len()
    out = "{out}lq"
  out

# ── H-7 `why(span)` ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Read-only, and it is the LSP hover. A role answers with its hue, its rendering
# facts, the rule that assigns it, the dnir edge it corresponds to, and every
# card that refines it. G-TOTAL requires every role to carry rule + why-chain;
# this is that chain, served from the same rows the projection reads.
gseventeen = "g17"

why: str = (key: str)
  r: str = rowof(key)
  out: str = "no role answers [{key}]"
  faint: str = "faint"
  if key == kbad
    cite: str = citing(gseventeen)
    out = "ambiguous  NO ROLE — two candidates, so a mixed-space diagnostic\n"
    out = "{out}  rule     {cite}\n"
    out = "{out}  dnir     dnir.none\n"
    out = "{out}  why      G-TOTAL forbids a default here; the span diagnoses\n"
  else(r != "")
    nm: str = field(r, 1)
    cd: str = field(r, 2)
    hu: str = field(r, 3)
    wt: str = field(r, 4)
    it: str = field(r, 5)
    lu: str = field(r, 6)
    cite: str = citing(field(r, 7))
    dn: str = field(r, 8)
    cap: str = field(r, 9)
    hx: str = hexof(hu)
    if lu == faint
      hx = hexof(faint)
    out = "{nm} [{cd}]  hue {hu} {hx}  weight {wt}  {it}  {lu}\n"
    out = "{out}  rule     {cite}\n"
    out = "{out}  dnir     {dn}\n"
    out = "{out}  capture  {cap}\n"
    i: i64 = 1
    while i <= cards:len()
      c: str = cards(i)
      if field(c, 2) == nm
        ccode: str = field(c, 3)
        cname: str = field(c, 1)
        out = "{out}  card     {ccode} {cname}"
        j: i64 = 4
        word: str = field(c, j)
        while word != ""
          out = "{out} {word}"
          j = j + 1
          word = field(c, j)
        out = "{out}\n"
      i = i + 1
  out

# The role and card at (line, byte) of a file, with its why-chain. Answers a
# diagnosis rather than a role where the projector declined to decide, because
# that IS the answer G-TOTAL wants a hover to give.
spanwhy: str = ()
  path: str = std.os.getenv("hlpath")
  ln: i64 = to(i64)(std.os.getenv("hlline"))
  col: i64 = to(i64)(std.os.getenv("hlbyte"))
  out: str = "why: needs hlpath, hlline and hlbyte"
  if path != "" and ln > 0 and col > 0
    f = io.open(path, "r")
    if not f
      out = "why: cannot read {path}"
    else
      body: str = f:read("a")
      f:close()
      surface: str = surfaceof(body)
      lines = body:split("\n")
      if ln > lines:len()
        out = "why: {path} has no line {ln}"
      else
        src: str = lines(ln)
        rr: str = project(src, surface)
        cc: str = carded(src, surface)
        if col > rr:len()
          out = "why: {path}:{ln} has no byte {col}"
        else
          rb: str = rr:sub(col, col)
          cb: str = cc:sub(col, col)
          glyph: str = src:sub(col, col)
          head: str = why(rb)
          cname: str = carding(rb, cb)
          out = "{path}:{ln}:{col}  [{glyph}]\n{head}"
          if cname != ""
            out = "{out}  HERE     card {cname}"
  out

# ── generated front-end artifacts ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# §0d: `highlights.scm` and the LSP legend are GENERATED; a hand edit is an H-1
# drift finding. Both come from the SAME rows, which is the only reason the two
# front-ends can be said to speak one colour language.

legend: str = ()
  out: str = ""
  i: i64 = 1
  while i <= roles:len()
    r: str = roles(i)
    mods: str = "-"
    if field(r, 4) == "heavy"
      mods = "declaration"
    if field(r, 5) == "italic"
      mods = "defaultlibrary"
    idx: str = to(str)(i - 1)
    nm: str = field(r, 1)
    out = "{out}{idx} {nm} {mods}\n"
    i = i + 1
  # without this the legend ends in a newline AND `print` adds one, so the
  # file carries one empty row past the last role. A consumer that indexes the
  # legend by position would be off by one at the end.
  if out:len() > 0
    out = out:sub(1, out:len() - 1)
  out

quote: str = ()
  "\""

# The tree-sitter capture for a role name. `closed` has already made an unknown
# role impossible, so the fallback is unreachable and says so by being dull.
capof: str = (name: str)
  out: str = field(rowof(name), 9)
  if out == ""
    out = "@variable"
  out

emitscm: str = ()
  q: str = quote()
  b: str = "; highlights.scm — GENERATED from the role taxonomy in\n"
  b = "{b}; tools/lsp/src/highlight.id. Do not edit this file by hand.\n"
  b = "{b};\n"
  b = "{b};     hlmode=scm duo run tools/lsp/src/highlight.id\n"
  b = "{b};\n"
  b = "{b}; CLAUDE.md §0d: highlights.scm and the LSP legend are GENERATED from the\n"
  b = "{b}; graph service; a hand edit is an H-1 drift finding. The taxonomy is the\n"
  b = "{b}; source of truth and this file is a LOSSY projection of it. Three losses,\n"
  b = "{b}; all counted by `zig build highlight-corpus` rather than hidden here:\n"
  b = "{b};\n"
  b = "{b};   1. tree-sitter sees ONE node for `:` and ONE for `|`, so the four roles\n"
  b = "{b};      H-1 names collapse to two captures. That is the baseline this whole\n"
  b = "{b};      artifact exists to beat, and the golden corpus is what convicts it.\n"
  b = "{b};   2. the fine splits of §0g have no capture vocabulary at all — a card is\n"
  b = "{b};      not expressible here, so `strata`, `param` and `group` parens are\n"
  b = "{b};      one capture and specificity through this front-end is lower than\n"
  b = "{b};      the projection's.\n"
  b = "{b};   3. there is no external scanner in ext/tree-sitter-duo/src, so there is\n"
  b = "{b};      no offside layer — see scripts/treesitter_emit.id.\n"
  b = "{b};\n"
  b = "{b}; role -> capture -> github dark (Primer) colour:\n"
  i: i64 = 1
  while i <= roles:len()
    r: str = roles(i)
    hx: str = hexof(field(r, 3))
    if field(r, 6) == "faint"
      hx = hexof("faint")
    nm: str = field(r, 1)
    cap: str = field(r, 9)
    b = "{b};   {nm} {cap} {hx}\n"
    i = i + 1
  b = "{b}\n"
  kcap: str = capof("keyword")
  b = "{b}[\n"
  for k, w in keywords:split(" ")
    b = "{b}  {q}{w}{q}\n"
  for k, w in legacy:split(" ")
    b = "{b}  {q}{w}{q}\n"
  b = "{b}] {kcap}\n\n"
  ycap: str = capof("case")
  b = "{b}[\n"
  for k, w in literals:split(" ")
    b = "{b}  {q}{w}{q}\n"
  b = "{b}] {ycap}\n\n"
  i = 1
  while i <= bindings:len()
    row: str = bindings(i)
    pat: str = field(row, 1)
    j: i64 = 2
    word: str = field(row, j)
    while field(row, j + 1) != ""
      pat = "{pat} {word}"
      j = j + 1
      word = field(row, j)
    cap: str = capof(word)
    at: i64 = pat:find("CAP")
    if at > 0
      pre: str = pat:sub(1, at - 1)
      post: str = pat:sub(at + 3, pat:len())
      b = "{b}{pre}{cap}{post}\n"
    else
      b = "{b}{pat} {cap}\n"
    i = i + 1
  ccap: str = capof("comment")
  b = "{b}\n(comment) {ccap} @spell\n\n"
  bcap: str = capof("layout")
  b = "{b}[\n  {q}({q} {q}){q}\n] {bcap}\n\n"
  b = "{b}[\n  {q},{q} {q}.{q}\n] {bcap}\n\n"
  # §4: the square brackets are the SPACE WALK, not layout. This is
  # the one place the generated query can carry a role the old grammar folded
  # into punctuation, so it does.
  rcap: str = capof("retrieve")
  b = "{b}[\n  {q}[{q} {q}]{q}\n] {rcap}\n\n"
  b = "{b}; THE H-1 HOLE, stated where an editor author will read it. `:` is ONE\n"
  b = "{b}; node to this grammar and so is `|`, so the four roles H-1 names —\n"
  b = "{b}; copula/invoke and union/pipe — cannot be separated by any query written\n"
  b = "{b}; here. The projection separates them and the golden corpus proves it;\n"
  b = "{b}; this front-end cannot carry the answer. That gap IS the measurement.\n"
  cop: str = capof("copula")
  ucap: str = capof("union")
  b = "{b}{q}:{q} {cop}\n"
  b = "{b}{q}|{q} {ucap}\n\n"
  ocap: str = capof("operator")
  b = "{b}[\n  {q}+{q} {q}-{q} {q}*{q} {q}/{q} {q}%{q} {q}^{q} {q}#{q}\n"
  b = "{b}  {q}=={q} {q}<{q} {q}>{q} {q}<={q} {q}>={q} {q}~={q} {q}!={q}\n"
  b = "{b}  {q}<<{q} {q}>>{q} {q}&{q} {q}~{q} {q}={q} {q}..{q}\n] {ocap}\n\n"
  b = "{b}[\n  {q}and{q} {q}or{q} {q}not{q} {q}in{q}\n] {kcap}\n"
  b

# The surface a fixture declares, from its `-- surface: <name>` header. Read as
# the THIRD FIELD of the line rather than as the text after the colon: `sub`
# past the colon keeps the leading space, and " ebnf" matches no surface name,
# so every non-idol fixture was silently scanned as idol and looked plausible.
# Only the FIRST header counts; a later line mentioning the word must not
# retarget the file.
surfaceof: str = (body: str)
  out: str = "idol"
  found: bool = false
  for i, ln in body:split("\n")
    if not found and ln:find("surface:") > 0
      out = field(ln, 3)
      found = true
  out

main: i64 = ()
  bad: str = closed()
  if bad != ""
    print("highlight: FAIL — {bad}")
    os.exit(3)

  mode: str = os.getenv("hlmode")
  if mode == ""
    mode = "roles"

  if mode == "table"
    print("roles, H-2 CLOSED — finite, enumerated, total; the count is a binding:")
    print("  name code hue weight italic luminance rule dnir capture")
    i: i64 = 1
    while i <= roles:len()
      r: str = roles(i)
      print("  {r}")
      i = i + 1
    print("cards, §0g fine splits — refinements WITHIN a role:")
    print("  name role code note")
    i = 1
    while i <= cards:len()
      c: str = cards(i)
      print("  {c}")
      i = i + 1
    os.exit(0)

  if mode == "legend"
    print(legend())
    os.exit(0)

  # H-7 `why(span)`, the read-only path. Given a FILE and a byte position it
  # projects, reads the role and card at that byte, and answers with the rule
  # citation and the dnir edge. `hlrole` still answers for a bare role, which
  # is what the generated legend needs; a SPAN is what a hover needs.
  if mode == "why"
    bare: str = os.getenv("hlrole")
    if bare != ""
      print(why(bare))
      os.exit(0)
    at: str = spanwhy()
    print(at)
    os.exit(0)

  if mode == "scm"
    print(emitscm())
    os.exit(0)

  # `hlpath` first: `duo run` hands the SCRIPT to argv, not the argument, so a
  # projector that only read argv answered "no path" under the interpreter and
  # correctly under a compiled binary. Env works under both.
  path: str = os.getenv("hlpath")
  if path == ""
    path = script.arg(1, "")
  if path == ""
    print("highlight: FAIL — no path; this mode needs a file")
    os.exit(2)
  f = io.open(path, "r")
  if not f
    print("highlight: FAIL — cannot read {path}")
    os.exit(2)
  body: str = f:read("a")
  f:close()
  surface: str = surfaceof(body)

  # A file ending in a newline splits to a trailing EMPTY element, and
  # printing a role line for it made the projector emit one line more than
  # the file has — which read, downstream, as every golden row being off by
  # one. Dropped here rather than in each consumer.
  lines = body:split("\n")
  last: i64 = lines:len()
  if last > 0 and lines(last) == ""
    last = last - 1
  i: i64 = 1
  while i <= last
    ln: str = lines(i)
    if mode == "cards"
      print(carded(ln, surface))
    else
      print(project(ln, surface))
    i = i + 1
  0
